From 85af75265fc675abfce04c2d0f50fc9dfe48bae6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 20:36:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] queue: v5 tier 1 (T8132) SRAM aperture is +0x94000

avd-fw-v5-t1 is only used by T8132, whose decoder has the same 0x14000-byte
code window as T6040: code at +0x80000, SRAM at +0x94000. The image
advertised 0x01092000, the value for a 0x12000-byte window, so the host
refuses the queue ("firmware SRAM aperture 0x1092000, node needs
0x1094000: wrong image tier") and stays on the legacy transport. The
information block itself is found, at the SRAM resource +0x7fc0, which
places SRAM at +0x94000.

Give tier 1 of the v5 generation the same aperture as tier 2. v4 keeps
0x01092000.

Tested on T8132 (MacBook Pro 14", M4) by loading the image at run time
before this change: the host found the AVQ1 block and refused the
aperture. Behaviour with this change: see the acceptance numbers.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 src/queue-fw.c | 3 +++
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/queue-fw.c b/src/queue-fw.c
index 0496376..1793a9a 100644
--- a/src/queue-fw.c
+++ b/src/queue-fw.c
@@ -24,6 +24,9 @@
 #elif AVD_VER == 5 && AVD_TIER == 2
 /* T6040/T6041: 0x14000-byte code window, so SRAM starts at +0x94000. */
 #define SRAM_APERTURE 0x01094000u
+#elif AVD_VER == 5 && AVD_TIER == 1
+/* T8132, the only v5-t1 user: also a 0x14000-byte code window (code +0x80000, sram +0x94000). */
+#define SRAM_APERTURE 0x01094000u
 #else
 #define SRAM_APERTURE 0x01092000u
 #endif
